    <!-- Body -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Running out of waterproofing coating halfway along the third wall is not merely annoying. It means a lap joint in the middle of a wall, a colour difference you will see forever, and a second trip that usually happens a week later when the enthusiasm has gone.
        </p>

        <div id="two-coat-rule" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Two-Coat Rule That Halves Every Number on the Label
          </h2>

          <p>
            Start here, because it is the single most common reason people underbuy. DRYLOK's waterproofing warranty is conditional on two coats. Not two coats if you feel like it, not two coats on the bad wall: two coats everywhere, brushed into the pores on the first pass. One coat is paint. Two coats is a waterproofing system, and the manufacturer is explicit that the performance claim applies to the latter.
          </p>

          <p>
            The coverage figure printed on the can is per coat. So when a gallon claims to cover 75 to 100 square feet, the number you should be working with for a real basement job is 37 to 50 square feet of finished wall per gallon. Everyone reads the first number and plans around it, and that is where the shortfall comes from.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a second reason the first coat goes further than you expect and the second coat goes less far than you expect, which confuses people mid-job. The first coat is absorbed into open masonry pores and disappears fast. The second coat sits on a partially sealed surface and covers considerably more area per gallon. If you budget both coats at the same rate you will over-buy slightly, which is the correct direction to be wrong in.
          </p>

          <p>
            The application method matters to the warranty as well. The first coat has to be worked into the surface with a stiff brush rather than rolled on, because the product waterproofs by filling and bridging the pores rather than by forming a film on top. Rolling the first coat leaves the pores open behind a skin, which is exactly the failure people describe when they say the coating bubbled off after a season.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Halve the label figure before you calculate anything.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A gallon that claims 100 square feet covers 50 square feet of finished, warranted wall. Brush the first coat, roll or brush the second. If you plan from the label number you will be roughly one gallon short on an average basement, and you will find out on the last wall.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="measuring" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Measuring the Wall Properly
          </h2>

          <p>
            The arithmetic is simple and people still get it wrong, usually by measuring the floor instead of the walls.
          </p>

          <p>
            You want wall area, not floor area. Measure the perimeter of the basement, in feet, and multiply by the height you are coating. For a rectangular basement the perimeter is twice the length plus twice the width. A 30 by 24 foot basement has a perimeter of 108 feet. At a full 8-foot wall height, that is 864 square feet of wall, which at 50 square feet per gallon of two-coat coverage is about 17 gallons. That is a substantially larger number than most people expect, and it is why the job is usually bought in five-gallon pails rather than gallons.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Basement</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Perimeter</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Wall Height</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Wall Area</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Two-Coat Gallons</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">20 x 24 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">88 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">616 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">12-16</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">24 x 30 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">108 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">864 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">17-23</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">28 x 36 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">128 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">8 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,024 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20-27</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">20 x 24 ft, 4 ft band only</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">88 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">352 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">7-9</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Decide the height honestly before you measure. Many people only coat the lower half of the wall, because that is where the damp is, and that is a legitimate choice for a damp-proofing job on a wall that is dry above grade. It is not legitimate if you want an even appearance, because the line where the coating stops is permanently visible. Coat to a natural break, such as the underside of the joists or a clean horizontal line you have chalked, rather than to the top of the damp.
          </p>

          <p>
            Subtract openings only if they are large. A standard basement window is about 8 square feet, and three of them come to 24 square feet, or roughly half a gallon. That is inside your rounding error and it is not worth the arithmetic. A walk-out door or a large window well opening is worth subtracting. Do not subtract for the area behind the furnace or the shelving you plan to move, because you will coat it anyway and you will be glad you did.
          </p>

          <p>
            Add area for anything the tape does not capture. A basement with pilasters, a chimney breast, or a bulkhead stairwell has meaningfully more surface than its perimeter suggests. Walk the room and add a rough square footage for each projection. Ten percent is a reasonable blanket allowance in an older house with an irregular foundation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="surface-type" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Surface Type Changes the Answer More Than Size Does
          </h2>

          <p>
            Two basements of identical area can need substantially different quantities, and the variable is texture. Coating fills pores and voids, so the rougher and more open the masonry, the more it drinks.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Surface</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">First-Coat Rate</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Second-Coat Rate</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Combined per Gallon</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Smooth poured concrete</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">125 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~55 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Parged / rendered wall</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">90 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">115 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~50 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Standard concrete block</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">75 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~43 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Rough or split-face block</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">50-60 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">85 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~34 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Old stone and mortar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">40-50 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">75 sq ft</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">~30 sq ft</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A rough block wall can take nearly twice as much material as smooth poured concrete over the same area. If your basement is split-face block or old rubble stone with deep mortar joints, plan from the bottom of that table and expect to be at the pessimistic end. The mortar joints alone on an old stone wall can account for a surprising share of the total, because they are both recessed and porous.
          </p>

          <p>
            Surface preparation changes the rate too, in both directions. Wire-brushing off loose material and efflorescence opens the pores and increases consumption on the first coat, which feels like a penalty and is actually the product doing its job. Skipping that preparation reduces consumption and also guarantees the coating fails, because it will not bond through a layer of powder. Our guide on efflorescence explains why that white bloom has to come off rather than be painted over.
          </p>

          <p>
            One surface where the calculation does not apply at all: previously painted walls. DRYLOK and similar masonry waterproofers need bare, clean masonry to bond and to fill the pores. Over existing paint they behave as a topcoat with no waterproofing function, and the warranty does not apply. If your walls are painted, the honest answer is that you are looking at a stripping job first, or a different product entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature and humidity matter less for coverage than for cure, but they do matter for workability. Coating applied in a cold basement is stiffer, goes on thicker, and covers less. The label conditions are typically above 50 degrees, and working below that both reduces coverage and compromises the cure.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="product-differences" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Which DRYLOK You Actually Bought
          </h2>

          <p>
            The range is wider than people notice on the shelf, and the products are not interchangeable. The coverage differs, the price differs by a lot, and one of them is not a wall product at all.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">DRYLOK Original Masonry Waterproofer</h3>

          <p>
            The entry product, latex-based, typically quoted around 100 square feet per gallon per coat on smooth masonry. It carries a shorter warranty than Extreme and a lower hydrostatic pressure rating. For a basement that is damp rather than actively seeping, it is adequate, and it is meaningfully cheaper per gallon.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">DRYLOK Extreme</h3>

          <p>
            The one most people should buy for a basement. Higher hydrostatic pressure rating, longer warranty, better mildew resistance in the film. Coverage is broadly similar to Original, in the 90 to 100 square foot range per coat on smooth masonry, so switching up does not change your gallon count much. The price difference is real but modest against the labour you are about to spend.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">DRYLOK Floor & Wall</h3>

          <p>
            Different product for a different job. It is formulated to take foot traffic, which the wall products are not, and the coverage rate is different again. Do not buy it to save money on walls, and do not put a wall product on a floor and expect it to survive being walked on. Our basement floor sealer guide covers what actually belongs down there.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">The Fast Plug Confusion</h3>

          <p>
            DRYLOK Fast Plug is hydraulic cement, not a coating. It comes in a tub, it sets in minutes, and it is for plugging an active leak or filling a hole before you coat. It is a consumable you will probably need and it does not go into the coverage calculation. Our hydraulic cement guide covers using it properly, because it is the product people most often ruin by mixing too much at once.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy all of your coating in one purchase and check the batch or lot numbers match where you can. Masonry waterproofers vary slightly between production batches, and a wall finished from a different batch can show a visible difference in a raking light. Boxing the material, which means combining all of it in a larger container and stirring before you start, eliminates the problem entirely and takes five minutes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="worked-examples" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Three Worked Examples, End to End
          </h2>

          <p>
            The method is clearer applied than described.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Example 1: Small Poured Basement, Full Height</h3>

          <p>
            A 20 by 24 foot basement with smooth poured walls, coating the full 7-foot height. Perimeter is 88 feet, wall area is 616 square feet. Smooth poured concrete gives roughly 55 square feet per gallon across two coats. That is 11.2 gallons, and rounding up for two pilasters and the chimney breast puts it at 12. Buy two five-gallon pails and two single gallons, which gives 12 gallons with a little margin.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Example 2: Mid-Size Block Basement, Full Height</h3>

          <p>
            A 24 by 30 foot basement in standard concrete block, full 8-foot height. Perimeter is 108 feet, wall area 864 square feet. Standard block gives about 43 square feet per gallon across two coats, so 20.1 gallons. Add ten percent for an irregular older foundation and you are at 22. That is four five-gallon pails plus two gallons. This is the case where people buy three pails, run out on the last wall, and discover the batch difference.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Example 3: Rough Block, Lower Band Only</h3>

          <p>
            A 28 by 36 foot basement in rough split-face block, coating only the lower 4 feet where the damp is. Perimeter is 128 feet, so the band is 512 square feet. Rough block is the expensive surface at roughly 34 square feet per gallon across two coats, giving 15.1 gallons, call it 16. Note that coating a quarter of the wall height in a large rough-block basement still costs more material than coating a small poured basement from floor to ceiling.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Where the Gallons Go</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Coats the warranty requires</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">~1.8x</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Rough block vs smooth poured</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">+10%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Allowance for older foundations</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Coverage over existing paint</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="other-materials" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. What Else the Job Consumes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Coating is the headline cost and it is not the whole shopping list. Missing one of these mid-job is the usual reason a weekend becomes two weekends.
          </p>

          <p>
            Brushes are a genuine consumable here, not a tool you keep. The first coat has to be scrubbed into masonry, which destroys a brush quickly, and masonry waterproofer is hard on bristles. Buy proper stiff-bristle masonry brushes and buy more than one. A 4-inch block brush is the usual choice; some people prefer a 6-inch for open walls and a smaller one for corners and joints.
          </p>

          <p>
            You will need a wire brush or a drill-mounted wire cup for preparation, and it is worth having both. Efflorescence, loose mortar and old flaking paint all have to come off, and the drill attachment turns a miserable day into a manageable one. Add a shop vac, because the dust from that preparation is considerable and it will otherwise end up in your coating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hydraulic cement for holes and active leaks, in a small tub, is close to mandatory. So is a masonry crack filler or an injection kit if you have a crack rather than general dampness. Coating over an unrepaired crack does not stop the crack, and the coating will fail there first. Our basement crack repair guide covers which repair suits which crack.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then the ordinary consumables: drop sheets, because this product is difficult to remove once dry; a paint scraper; rags; and eye protection, which matters more than usual because you are scrubbing a stiff brush overhead into masonry. Add a respirator rated for the dust if you are doing significant wire brushing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Budget a full day for preparation on an average basement before a drop of coating is opened. That ratio surprises people, but the preparation is what decides whether the coating holds, and it is the step that gets compressed when the schedule slips. Our full basement waterproofing supplies checklist lists everything in one place.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Buying, Rounding, and What to Do With the Leftovers
          </h2>

          <p>
            A few practical decisions at the till that save money or trouble.
          </p>

          <p>
            Always round up, and round up to the next pail rather than the next gallon if you are within about three gallons of a pail. Five-gallon pails are consistently cheaper per gallon than singles, often by a wide margin, and the marginal cost of having a gallon left over is far lower than the cost of a second trip plus a batch mismatch on a visible wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            Box the material before you start. Combine the pails in a clean larger container, or at minimum pour a portion of each into a working bucket and top it up alternately, so that any batch variation is distributed evenly across the whole basement rather than concentrated on one wall. This is standard practice in the trades and almost unknown among homeowners.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do not thin it. Masonry waterproofer is formulated to be applied at full body, and thinning it to stretch coverage or make brushing easier defeats the pore-filling mechanism that makes it a waterproofer rather than a paint. If it is too stiff to work, the basement is too cold, and the answer is to wait for warmer weather or heat the space.
          </p>

          <p>
            Leftovers store reasonably well in a sealed container kept from freezing, which a basement usually provides. Keep at least a gallon for touch-ups, because you will find spots you missed once the walls dry and the colour evens out. Label it with the date and the room, since an unmarked pail in a basement in three years is a mystery nobody solves.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, do not treat the coating as the whole project. A masonry waterproofer manages dampness and minor seepage on the inside face of a wall. It does not stop water arriving under hydrostatic pressure, and it does not address the reason water is at the wall in the first place. Extending downspouts and correcting the grade outside costs almost nothing and reduces the load on everything you are about to apply.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Planning From the Label Coverage Figure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The number on the can is per coat, and the waterproofing warranty requires two. Work from roughly half the label figure for finished wall area. This single error is why most people end up one pail short on the final wall, with a visible lap joint and a batch mismatch to show for it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Rolling the First Coat Instead of Brushing It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The product waterproofs by filling and bridging masonry pores, not by forming a film on top. A rolled first coat leaves the pores open behind a skin, which is precisely the failure people describe as the coating bubbling or peeling off after one season. Scrub the first coat in with a stiff masonry brush.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Applying It Over Existing Paint</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Masonry waterproofers need bare, clean masonry to bond and to fill pores. Over paint they act as a decorative topcoat with no waterproofing function and no warranty. If the walls are already painted, you are facing a stripping job or a different product, not a straightforward recoat.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Measuring Floor Area Instead of Wall Area</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                You are coating walls, so the calculation is perimeter multiplied by the height you intend to coat. A 24 by 30 basement has 720 square feet of floor and 864 square feet of wall at 8 feet. Using the floor figure understates the job by a fifth before the two-coat rule is even applied.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Pails Separately and Not Boxing Them</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Masonry waterproofer varies slightly between production batches, and a wall finished from a different batch shows a visible difference in raking light. Combining all the material and stirring before you start distributes any variation evenly across the basement. It takes five minutes and cannot be undone afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much DRYLOK do I need for a 1,000 square foot basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                That figure is usually floor area, and you coat walls. A basement with 1,000 square feet of floor is roughly 25 by 40 feet, giving a 130-foot perimeter and about 1,040 square feet of wall at 8 feet. On standard block at two coats that is roughly 24 gallons, or five pails. On smooth poured concrete it is closer to 19.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many square feet does a gallon of DRYLOK cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The label typically says 75 to 100 square feet per gallon per coat on smooth masonry. Because the waterproofing warranty requires two coats, the figure to plan with is about 37 to 50 square feet of finished wall. On rough or split-face block, plan closer to 34 square feet per gallon across both coats.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I really need two coats of DRYLOK?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you want the product to waterproof and the warranty to apply. One coat is decorative masonry paint. Two coats, with the first scrubbed into the pores with a stiff brush, is the waterproofing system the manufacturer tested and warrants. Skipping the second coat to stretch material is the most expensive saving available here.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I put DRYLOK over painted basement walls?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No, not as a waterproofer. It needs bare, clean masonry to bond into and to fill the pores that carry moisture. Applied over paint it behaves as a topcoat, provides no waterproofing, and voids the warranty. The wall has to be stripped back to sound masonry first, which is a significant job worth pricing before you commit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is DRYLOK Extreme worth it over DRYLOK Original?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a basement, usually yes. Extreme carries a higher hydrostatic pressure rating, a longer warranty and better mildew resistance in the film, and its coverage is broadly similar, so it does not change your gallon count much. The price difference is modest compared with the labour of preparing and coating a basement twice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between DRYLOK and DRYLOK Fast Plug?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are unrelated products. DRYLOK is a brush-applied masonry waterproofing coating sold in gallons and pails. Fast Plug is hydraulic cement, sold as a powder in a tub, that sets in a few minutes and is used to plug active leaks and fill holes before coating. You will probably need both, and Fast Plug is not part of the coverage calculation.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I coat the whole wall or just the damp lower section?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Coating only the lower band is a legitimate choice on a wall that is genuinely dry above grade, and it saves real material. The downside is that the line where the coating stops stays visible permanently. If you do it, stop at a natural break such as the underside of the joists or a chalked line, not at the ragged top edge of the damp.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

