        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Halve the label coverage, measure wall area rather than floor area, and pick your rate from the surface you actually have rather than the smooth concrete the label assumes. For an average block basement at full height that lands somewhere between 20 and 25 gallons, which is four to five pails, and it is a considerably bigger number than most people walk into the store expecting.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Round up to the next pail, box the material before you start so batch variation is spread across the whole basement, and never thin it to stretch coverage. And remember what the coating is for: it manages dampness and minor seepage on the inside face of a wall. Extending the downspouts and fixing the grade outside costs almost nothing and reduces the load on every gallon you are about to apply.
          </p>
        </div>

